  "style-vs-theme": "**Direct answer:**\nA Style is a collection of attribute values for a specific view (e.g., custom text style). A Theme is a style applied to an entire activity or application, affecting all views.\n\n**Why this is asked:**\nExplain style vs theme. Interviewers use this question to test whether you understand the underlying mechanism, not just the terminology -- so go beyond the definition and explain where it fits in an Android app's architecture or lifecycle, the trade-offs involved, and a concrete scenario where it matters.\n\n**Breaking it down:**\n(1) A Style is a collection of attribute values for a specific view (e.g., custom text style). (2) A Theme is a style applied to an entire activity or application, affecting all views. Each of these points builds on the previous one: start with the core definition, then explain the mechanics of how it works under the hood (lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage where relevant), and finish with the practical consequence a developer would actually observe (a crash, a memory leak, a lifecycle bug, or a UX difference).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:**\n1) State the definition in one clear sentence. 2) Explain the why -- the problem this Android concept or API solves. 3) Give a short, concrete code snippet or API call to illustrate it, preferring current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns. 4) Mention one common pitfall, deprecated alternative, or version/API-level difference the interviewer might probe next, to show hands-on depth beyond memorized theory.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:**\nInterviewers frequently pivot from a question like this one into asking you to compare it against a closely related alternative, ask about a real bug or crash you've hit involving it, or ask how you'd test or verify the behavior -- so be ready to connect this answer to a concrete example from your own project experience.",
