  "permissions": "**Direct answer:**\nandroid.permission.INTERNET allows the app to open network sockets and is a normal permission granted at install time. android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE lets the app query information about network connectivity and state; it is also a normal permission. ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is not required merely to make an HTTP request. Therefore, an app making network calls normally needs INTERNET, while it needs ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE only when it also needs to inspect network state.\n\n**Why this is asked:**\nExplain permissions. Interviewers use this question to test whether you understand the underlying mechanism, not just the terminology -- so go beyond the definition and explain where it fits in an Android app's architecture or lifecycle, the trade-offs involved, and a concrete scenario where it matters.\n\n**Breaking it down:**\n(1) android.permission.INTERNET allows the app to open network sockets and is a normal permission granted at install time. (2) android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE lets the app query information about network connectivity and state; it is also a normal permission. (3) ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is not required merely to make an HTTP request. (4) Therefore, an app making network calls normally needs INTERNET, while it needs ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE only when it also needs to inspect network state. Each of these points builds on the previous one: start with the core definition, then explain the mechanics of how it works under the hood (lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage where relevant), and finish with the practical consequence a developer would actually observe (a crash, a memory leak, a lifecycle bug, or a UX difference).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:**\n1) State the definition in one clear sentence. 2) Explain the why -- the problem this Android concept or API solves. 3) Give a short, concrete code snippet or API call to illustrate it, preferring current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns. 4) Mention one common pitfall, deprecated alternative, or version/API-level difference the interviewer might probe next, to show hands-on depth beyond memorized theory.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:**\nInterviewers frequently pivot from a question like this one into asking you to compare it against a closely related alternative, ask about a real bug or crash you've hit involving it, or ask how you'd test or verify the behavior -- so be ready to connect this answer to a concrete example from your own project experience.",
  "dangerous-permissions": "**Direct answer:**\nAndroid classifies permissions by protection level. Dangerous permissions protect sensitive data or actions, such as camera, location, microphone, and certain storage/media access, and many must be requested at runtime on Android 6.0/API 23 and later when the app targets the relevant API levels. The exact permission set and runtime behavior can change across Android releases, so applications should request only the permissions they actually need and explain the user-facing purpose before requesting them.\n\n**Why this is asked:**\nExplain dangerous permissions. Interviewers use this question to test whether you understand the underlying mechanism, not just the terminology -- so go beyond the definition and explain where it fits in an Android app's architecture or lifecycle, the trade-offs involved, and a concrete scenario where it matters.\n\n**Breaking it down:**\n(1) Android classifies permissions by protection level. (2) Dangerous permissions protect sensitive data or actions, such as camera, location, microphone, and certain storage/media access, and many must be requested at runtime on Android 6.0/API 23 and later when the app targets the relevant API levels. (3) The exact permission set and runtime behavior can change across Android releases, so applications should request only the permissions they actually need and explain the user-facing purpose before requesting them. Each of these points builds on the previous one: start with the core definition, then explain the mechanics of how it works under the hood (lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage where relevant), and finish with the practical consequence a developer would actually observe (a crash, a memory leak, a lifecycle bug, or a UX difference).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:**\n1) State the definition in one clear sentence. 2) Explain the why -- the problem this Android concept or API solves. 3) Give a short, concrete code snippet or API call to illustrate it, preferring current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns. 4) Mention one common pitfall, deprecated alternative, or version/API-level difference the interviewer might probe next, to show hands-on depth beyond memorized theory.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:**\nInterviewers frequently pivot from a question like this one into asking you to compare it against a closely related alternative, ask about a real bug or crash you've hit involving it, or ask how you'd test or verify the behavior -- so be ready to connect this answer to a concrete example from your own project experience.",
