  "protection-level": "**Direct answer:**\n`protectionLevel` defines the permission type: `normal`, `dangerous`, `signature`, `signatureOrSystem`. It indicates how the permission is granted.\n\n**Why this is asked:**\nExplain protection level. Interviewers use this question to test whether you understand the underlying mechanism, not just the terminology -- so go beyond the definition and explain where it fits in an Android app's architecture or lifecycle, the trade-offs involved, and a concrete scenario where it matters.\n\n**Breaking it down:**\n(1) `protectionLevel` defines the permission type: `normal`, `dangerous`, `signature`, `signatureOrSystem`. (2) It indicates how the permission is granted. Each of these points builds on the previous one: start with the core definition, then explain the mechanics of how it works under the hood (lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage where relevant), and finish with the practical consequence a developer would actually observe (a crash, a memory leak, a lifecycle bug, or a UX difference).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:**\n1) State the definition in one clear sentence. 2) Explain the why -- the problem this Android concept or API solves. 3) Give a short, concrete code snippet or API call to illustrate it, preferring current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns. 4) Mention one common pitfall, deprecated alternative, or version/API-level difference the interviewer might probe next, to show hands-on depth beyond memorized theory.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:**\nInterviewers frequently pivot from a question like this one into asking you to compare it against a closely related alternative, ask about a real bug or crash you've hit involving it, or ask how you'd test or verify the behavior -- so be ready to connect this answer to a concrete example from your own project experience.",
  "secure-password-storage": "**Direct answer:**\nAn Android app should not store a user's plaintext password, and simply encrypting a password in local storage does not make password storage a good authentication design. In a server-backed system, send credentials over HTTPS to the authentication service and let the server store passwords using a strong password-hashing scheme. On the device, store only the minimum authentication material needed, such as a short-lived token or refresh credential, protected appropriately. Android Keystore is useful for protecting cryptographic keys; it is not a password database.\n\n**Why this is asked:**\nExplain secure password storage. Interviewers use this question to test whether you understand the underlying mechanism, not just the terminology -- so go beyond the definition and explain where it fits in an Android app's architecture or lifecycle, the trade-offs involved, and a concrete scenario where it matters.\n\n**Breaking it down:**\n(1) An Android app should not store a user's plaintext password, and simply encrypting a password in local storage does not make password storage a good authentication design. (2) In a server-backed system, send credentials over HTTPS to the authentication service and let the server store passwords using a strong password-hashing scheme. (3) On the device, store only the minimum authentication material needed, such as a short-lived token or refresh credential, protected appropriately. (4) Android Keystore is useful for protecting cryptographic keys; it is not a password database. Each of these points builds on the previous one: start with the core definition, then explain the mechanics of how it works under the hood (lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage where relevant), and finish with the practical consequence a developer would actually observe (a crash, a memory leak, a lifecycle bug, or a UX difference).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:**\n1) State the definition in one clear sentence. 2) Explain the why -- the problem this Android concept or API solves. 3) Give a short, concrete code snippet or API call to illustrate it, preferring current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns. 4) Mention one common pitfall, deprecated alternative, or version/API-level difference the interviewer might probe next, to show hands-on depth beyond memorized theory.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:**\nInterviewers frequently pivot from a question like this one into asking you to compare it against a closely related alternative, ask about a real bug or crash you've hit involving it, or ask how you'd test or verify the behavior -- so be ready to connect this answer to a concrete example from your own project experience.",
  "allowbackup": "**Direct answer:**\nandroid:allowBackup controls whether the application participates in Android backup and restore mechanisms, but backup behavior has evolved across Android versions and device manufacturers. Do not claim that setting it to false is automatically the correct security policy for every application. Instead, define explicit backup rules for sensitive data where supported, avoid placing secrets in backupable locations, and review the current Android backup documentation for the target SDK and device behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked:**\nExplain allowbackup. Interviewers use this question to test whether you understand the underlying mechanism, not just the terminology -- so go beyond the definition and explain where it fits in an Android app's architecture or lifecycle, the trade-offs involved, and a concrete scenario where it matters.\n\n**Breaking it down:**\n(1) android:allowBackup controls whether the application participates in Android backup and restore mechanisms, but backup behavior has evolved across Android versions and device manufacturers. (2) Do not claim that setting it to false is automatically the correct security policy for every application. (3) Instead, define explicit backup rules for sensitive data where supported, avoid placing secrets in backupable locations, and review the current Android backup documentation for the target SDK and device behavior. Each of these points builds on the previous one: start with the core definition, then explain the mechanics of how it works under the hood (lifecycle ownership, threading, state restoration, security, or resource usage where relevant), and finish with the practical consequence a developer would actually observe (a crash, a memory leak, a lifecycle bug, or a UX difference).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer:**\n1) State the definition in one clear sentence. 2) Explain the why -- the problem this Android concept or API solves. 3) Give a short, concrete code snippet or API call to illustrate it, preferring current AndroidX/Jetpack APIs and Kotlin patterns. 4) Mention one common pitfall, deprecated alternative, or version/API-level difference the interviewer might probe next, to show hands-on depth beyond memorized theory.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate:**\nInterviewers frequently pivot from a question like this one into asking you to compare it against a closely related alternative, ask about a real bug or crash you've hit involving it, or ask how you'd test or verify the behavior -- so be ready to connect this answer to a concrete example from your own project experience.",
